[{:db/id -1, :adr/id "adr-2605312300-giemon-kabitori-mold-removal-probe-sim", :adr/title "ADR-2605312300: giemon kabitori (黴取り) mold-removal probe — sim + wall-obstacle contact extension + mold-erosion field", :adr/status "accepted", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/body "\n# ADR-2605312300: giemon kabitori (黴取り) mold-removal probe — sim + wall-obstacle contact extension + mold-erosion field\n\n**Status**: accepted\n\n## Context\n\nThe founder asked (2026-05-31, JP): how far has \"nano machine\" research progressed, and can we **design + implement a machine to remove existing mold** from A/C units (coils, drain pans, blower housings), building gaps/cavities, and HVAC ducts — explicitly relaxing scale to micro/milli, and explicitly asking to **simulate it on giemon + kami-engine** (the clean-room Omniverse/PhysX/Isaac-class stack, ADR-2605261800).\n\nHonest framing established up front: an autonomous \"nanomachine\" that crawls in and eats mold is science fiction (no air-borne propulsion, autonomy, energy, or recovery). The achievable machine is a **steerable, suction-coupled rotary-brush probe**. The binding constraints are not the machine's smallness but: (a) **gap geometry** (A/C fin pitch ~1 mm is unreachable; drain pans / blower / ducts / wall cavities are reachable), (b) **removal physics** (must kill AND mechanically detach AND extract — dead mold is still allergenic), and (c) **spore containment** (scrub with suction, never re-aerosolise).\n\nThe kami-genesis engine (ADR-2605311800) provides rigid articulated-body dynamics (RNEA+CRBA+LDLᵀ, revolute/prismatic/arbitrary-axis) + a sequential-impulse contact solver with Coulomb friction — but, at R1.1, contact was **only against a single horizontal ground plane**, and there is **no erodible-material solver** (the Genesis MPM/FEM/SPH/PBD solvers are deferred). So a faithful sim of \"reach into a boxed-in duct and scrub mold off a wall\" needed two additions.\n\n## Decision\n\nBuild the machine as a **giemon variant** (`giemon kabitori`) on kami-genesis, and make two scoped engine/model additions. All verified by direct `cargo test`.\n\n1. **Probe articulation** — `70-tools/e7m-sim/scenes/giemon_kabitori/giemon_kabitori.urdf`: a 6-DOF mixed-topology arm — `j_feed` (PRISMATIC +x, insert into the gap 0–300 mm) → `j_yaw` (REVOLUTE +z) → `j_pitch` (REVOLUTE +y, dip onto the surface) → `j_seg1`,`j_seg2` (REVOLUTE +y, flexible-segment continuum approximation) → `j_brush` (REVOLUTE +x, rotary brush spin). Loaded via the validated `from_articulated_system` path (routes to the `Spatial3d` solver). Code in `40-engine/kami-engine/kami-app-giemon/src/lib.rs`: `giemon_kabitori_config`, `kabitori_colliders` (brush \"bristle cross\" of capsules + a distal-shaft capsule), and the WASM render entry `run_giemon_kabitori_sim_v1` (autonomous feed→dip→scrub demo).\n\n2. **Engine extension — static environment obstacles** (`kami-genesis/src/contact.rs`): new `Obstacle::Plane { normal, offset }` (solid half-space) and `Obstacle::Aabb { min, max }` (solid box, nearest-face push-out), held on `ContactWorld` via the additive `with_obstacles(...)` builder. The PGS solver already resolves an arbitrary contact normal, so only `generate()` and the struct changed — obstacles compose with the implicit ground plane at no solver-architecture cost and **zero behavioural change when the obstacle list is empty** (backward compatible). This lets a scene box-in a duct / gap / cavity (floor + side walls + back wall).\n\n3. **Application-layer mold-erosion field** (`kami-app-giemon/src/mold_field.rs`): a 2-D coverage grid `MoldField` lying on the surface plane, coverage ∈ [0,1] per cell. The driver `kabitori_scrub_step` advances one contact step and then erodes coverage under the brush proportional to **scrub work** (tangential slip speed of the brush contact point × dt, with a pressure proxy), localised to the brush footprint, saturating at 0. This models the mold as removable material **at the application layer**, consuming only public articulation state — it does **not** couple into the solver, and is clearly labelled as a stand-in until the deferred fluid/erodible-material solvers land.\n\n4. **Hardware BOM (R0)** — `scenes/giemon_kabitori/BOM.md`: flexible probe shaft + tendon steering + linear feed + flex-shaft brush drive + **coaxial suction + H13 HEPA + collection tank + registered biocide mist** (the spore-containment elements the rigid sim does not yet model) + tip camera + MCU. Includes a safety preamble and a de-risked build order whose make-or-break first test is \"brush + suction shroud removes mold from a coupon without aerosol escape\", and an explicit sim↔hardware correspondence table.\n\n## Consequences\n\n**Verified (direct cargo-test observation, 2026-05-31):**\n- `kami-genesis`: **94 passed / 0 failed** (the prior suite + two new obstacle tests: a side-wall half-space confines a swinging link; a sphere-vs-AABB yields the correct outward contact). All pre-existing behaviour unchanged (obstacle list defaults empty).\n- `kami-app-giemon`: **12 passed / 0 failed**, including: mixed-topology parse (6 DOF = 1 prismatic + 5 revolute); feed inserts the carriage into the gap; brush reaches + scrubs the surface with no tunnelling; contact is passive once settled (no Baumgarte energy pumping); **scrubbing erodes the mold field locally** (total coverage drops, a far corner stays full); and **gap-wall obstacles confine the probe** under an aggressive yaw sweep. `mold_field` unit tests confirm scrub is local, bounded, and non-negative.\n- WASM: `kami-app-giemon` `cargo check --target wasm32-unknown-unknown` is green (the demo renders a walled gap + a live mold grid recoloured by remaining coverage as the brush scrubs).\n- The obstacle API is already independently consumed by `kami-app-shibuya` (buildings → `Obstacle::Aabb`), confirming the extension generalises.\n\n**Honest limits (explicitly NOT claimed):** the mold biofilm is not a physically erodible material (no FEM/MPM/SPH in kami-genesis R1.1) — `MoldField` is a kinematic coverage proxy; contact remains link-vs-static-environment only (no self-collision, no dynamic-dynamic); spray/airflow/suction (BOM group C) are unmodelled; the continuum probe is a discrete-revolute approximation, not a true rod. The sim validates **reach, contact-force regulation, scrub shear, gap confinement, and localised removal** — i.e. the robotics — not the chemistry/fluidics.\n\n**Build note:** on this host Homebrew rust shadows rustup on `PATH` and lacks the wasm32 std; wasm builds must pin `RUSTC` to the rustup toolchain compiler (documented in the scene README).\n\n**Constitutional:** clean-room throughout (no NVIDIA / PhysX / Isaac code; ADR-2605261800 N1..N9). No inference paths touched. Additive engine change behind a builder; no API removed.\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n- **\"Nanomachine\" as literally requested** — rejected as non-existent technology; reframed to a steerable probe with an explicit science-vs-fiction table. (The conversation also covered the realistic nanotech that *does* exist — photocatalytic TiO₂ / Ag-nanoparticle antifungal coatings — but those are prevention, not removal of existing mold, so out of scope for this build.)\n- **A self-driving in-pipe crawler robot** — viable for large ducts but overkill and unreachable for fins/narrow gaps; the steerable probe covers more target environments at R0. Left as a future `giemon` variant.\n- **Erosion inside the solver (new FEM/MPM material)** — correct long-term, but a major engine effort; deferred. The application-layer `MoldField` gives the \"mold disappears where scrubbed\" result now, cleanly separated so a future solver can replace it.\n- **A standalone `kami-app-kabitori` crate** — rejected for R0 in favour of extending `kami-app-giemon` (giemon is the repo's robotics-kit brand; deps + the proven physics-arm pattern are already wired; additive, no merge-gate per kami-engine CLAUDE.md).\n- **AABB-only vs plane-only obstacles** — implemented both: planes are the workhorse for gap/duct walls, AABB for solid bodies (and immediately reused by shibuya for buildings).\n\n## References\n\n- Probe articulation + colliders + scrub driver + WASM entry: `40-engine/kami-engine/kami-app-giemon/src/lib.rs`\n- Mold-erosion field: `40-engine/kami-engine/kami-app-giemon/src/mold_field.rs`\n- Engine obstacle extension: `40-engine/kami-engine/kami-genesis/src/contact.rs` (`Obstacle`, `ContactWorld::with_obstacles`)\n- Scene + design + BOM: `70-tools/e7m-sim/scenes/giemon_kabitori/{giemon_kabitori.urdf, README.edn, BOM.md}`\n- Physics engine: ADR-2605311800 (3-D spatial articulation + contact), ADR-2605311500 (validation + Isaac clean-room surface)\n- Stack policy: ADR-2605261800 (Omniverse/PhysX/Isaac clean-room compat, N1..N9), ADR-2605261600 (e7m-sim robotics substrate R0)\n", :adr/path "90-docs/adr/2605312300-giemon-kabitori-mold-removal-probe-sim.edn", :adr/source-repo "root", :adr/topic "giemon-kabitori-mold-removal-sim", :adr/last_verified "2026-05-31", :adr/date "2026-05-31", :adr/authoritative true, :adr/authoritative_for ["giemon kabitori mold-removal probe articulation (6-DOF mixed prismatic+revolute) + scene URDF" "kami-genesis static environment obstacles (Obstacle::Plane / Obstacle::Aabb) in the contact solver" "kabitori mold-coverage erosion model (MoldField) at the giemon application layer" "honest scope boundary: rigid-body + ground/wall contact only; no biofilm/fluid solver yet"]}]
